@OpenTelemetry.Api_HostAddress = http://localhost:5038

### Create a store. Watch the console: one activity, its traceId invented by .NET.
POST {{OpenTelemetry.Api_HostAddress}}/stores
Content-Type: application/json

{
  "name": "Jakarta Pusat Flagship",
  "address": "Jl. M.H. Thamrin No. 1, Jakarta"
}

### The same request, this time carrying a trace context of our own.
# Format: version-traceId-parentSpanId-flags
# The console should print traceId=4bf92f3577b34da6a3ce929d0e0e4736 and
# parentId=00f067aa0ba902b7. Nothing in Program.cs parses this header —
# ASP.NET Core does it, per the W3C Trace Context spec.
POST {{OpenTelemetry.Api_HostAddress}}/stores
Content-Type: application/json
traceparent: 00-4bf92f3577b34da6a3ce929d0e0e4736-00f067aa0ba902b7-01

{
  "name": "Bandung Outlet",
  "address": "Jl. Asia Afrika No. 8, Bandung"
}

### Read one store back. Replace the id with one returned above.
GET {{OpenTelemetry.Api_HostAddress}}/stores/00000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000000
Accept: application/json

### List every store.
GET {{OpenTelemetry.Api_HostAddress}}/stores
Accept: application/json

### Throw on purpose. GlobalExceptionHandler catches it, marks the span Error
### and returns RFC 9457 ProblemDetails.
###
### Phase 4: check the response headers. The traceparent here is written by
### GlobalExceptionHandler, not by the middleware — UseExceptionHandler called
### Response.Clear() and wiped the middleware's copy on the way past. The header
### and the body's traceId should be byte-for-byte identical.
GET {{OpenTelemetry.Api_HostAddress}}/stores/00000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000000/boom
Accept: application/json

### Phase 4. Send no traceparent and read the one that comes back. .NET invents
### a trace id, and the header's second field matches the console's spanId.
GET {{OpenTelemetry.Api_HostAddress}}/stores
Accept: application/json

### Phase 4. Send a traceparent and compare it with the response's.
### Field 2 (trace id) comes back unchanged — same journey.
### Field 3 (span id) is different — the server span is a new participant in it.
GET {{OpenTelemetry.Api_HostAddress}}/stores
Accept: application/json
traceparent: 00-4bf92f3577b34da6a3ce929d0e0e4736-00f067aa0ba902b7-01

### Phase 6. A resolvable address. The trace now has three spans: the request
### span, CreateStore, and a CLIENT span for the call to Nominatim that nothing
### in Program.cs created. Check store.address on CreateStore against url.full
### on the client span — the query string there is redacted.
POST {{OpenTelemetry.Api_HostAddress}}/stores
Content-Type: application/json

{
  "name": "Brandenburg Gate Kiosk",
  "address": "Brandenburg Gate, Berlin"
}

### Phase 6. An address Nominatim cannot resolve. It answers 200 with an empty
### array, so the failure is ours to detect: geocode.match_count is 0 and the
### handler throws. GlobalExceptionHandler turns that into ProblemDetails and
### marks the span Error — no new error-handling code in this phase.
POST {{OpenTelemetry.Api_HostAddress}}/stores
Content-Type: application/json

{
  "name": "Nowhere Outlet",
  "address": "qwertyuiop asdfghjkl zxcvbnm 12345"
}

### Phase 6. Send a traceparent of your own and watch it survive the hop. The
### client span shares this trace id, and the header Nominatim receives carries
### it too — written by AddHttpClientInstrumentation, not by us.
POST {{OpenTelemetry.Api_HostAddress}}/stores
Content-Type: application/json
traceparent: 00-4bf92f3577b34da6a3ce929d0e0e4736-00f067aa0ba902b7-01

{
  "name": "Traced Store",
  "address": "Eiffel Tower, Paris"
}

###
